 {
  "id": "L21/p3",
  "lesson": "L21",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>DPO and RLHF with PPO are trained on the same preference data with the same <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0528em;\">β</span></span></span></span>. In the limit of unlimited data and optimization, their policies:</p>",
  "answer": "b",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>Differ, because DPO has no reward model.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>Are the same: both reach the KL-regularized optimum of the Bradley-Terry reward.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>Differ, because DPO cannot use a KL penalty.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>Are the same only if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0528em;\">β</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span>.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>DPO is derived from the same objective: its loss is the Bradley-Terry likelihood written in terms of the policy that would be optimal for that reward. In practice they differ because data and optimization are finite, PPO samples new responses while DPO only sees the dataset, and the reward model generalizes differently from the implicit one.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L21/p4",
  "lesson": "L21",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>You remove the KL penalty from an RL run with a reward that is correct but sparse. After many steps, the policy's entropy is:</p>",
  "answer": "d",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>Unchanged.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>Higher, because the policy explores more.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>Exactly that of the reference.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>Near zero: it answers every prompt the same way, and has stopped exploring alternatives.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>Policy gradients concentrate probability on whatever earned reward, and nothing pushes back. The policy collapses toward deterministic answers. This can look fine on the training reward, but diversity is gone, later training has nothing to explore, and on imperfect rewards the collapse is onto the exploit. Exercise 12 measures it.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L21/concept1",
  "lesson": "L21",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What is the policy gradient?",
  "answer": "The gradient of expected reward equals the expectation of reward times the gradient of the log-probability of the sampled response, so it can be estimated from samples.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L21/concept2",
  "lesson": "L21",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why does a baseline not bias the policy gradient?",
  "answer": "The expected score function is zero, so subtracting any baseline that does not depend on the sampled response leaves the expectation unchanged while reducing variance.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L21/concept3",
  "lesson": "L21",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What is the optimum of reward minus beta times KL to a reference?",
  "answer": "The reference policy tilted by exp(reward / beta) and renormalized.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L21/concept4",
  "lesson": "L21",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "How does DPO train on preferences without a reward model?",
  "answer": "It writes the reward as beta times the log-ratio of policy to reference, substitutes it into the Bradley-Terry likelihood, and maximizes that likelihood directly.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L21/concept5",
  "lesson": "L21",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What are group-relative advantages?",
  "answer": "Several responses are sampled for the same prompt and each reward is normalized by the group's mean and standard deviation, replacing a value network as the baseline.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L21/concept6",
  "lesson": "L21",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What is reward overoptimization?",
  "answer": "Optimizing a proxy reward too hard raises the proxy while true quality first rises and then falls, which a KL penalty and held-out evaluations guard against.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
